nodecode-clearance is a rewrite of yuzuru99's yuzuru99/hermes-clearance:

  https://github.com/yuzuru99/hermes-clearance
  commit 40adfd6db365d1279733ca541e8c25383302d74e
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 yuzuru99 (LICENSE)

The original is a Python plugin for Hermes: a pre-tool-call hook that reads the
sender's platform and user id out of the gateway's session environment, a
pre-llm-call hook that applies an hourly rate limit and appends a line naming
the sender's tier to the turn, and a /clearance command. This rewrite keeps the
policy — five built-in tiers, deny-by-default tool decisions where deny beats
allow, groups and globs over tool names, per-sender assignments with platform
and everyone wildcards, per-tier hourly limits, an owner's audit trail of
refused calls with argument names and never their values, and a /clearance
command that reports and assigns — and reimplements it in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's cell contract: one :tool hook, one slash command, one manual topic
and a `clearance` config section. What the original read from environment
variables, this reads from the session the running turn belongs to and from the
channel kit's lane table, so a room sender is attributed by the room itself and
a session no channel claims is the operator's own. The group names are
Nodecode's tools rather than Hermes's (eval is the one tool that reaches the
whole machine), the `users` setting is a list of `platform:user_id=tier`
entries because a section is typed data here, and /clearance set writes that
list through Nodecode's config writer. Left out: the per-turn "this sender is
X" note the original injected into the model's context, since a tool hook here
cannot add to a prompt; the tier-change notice that went with it; and the
`blocked` tier's turn refusal, which this cell enforces by refusing every tool
the tier names, because Nodecode's chat lanes open turns the cell does not own.
